#: src/development.md:11
msgid "Keychain access"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:13
msgid "Zed stores secrets in the system keychain."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:15
msgid ""
"However, when running a development build of Zed on macOS (and perhaps other "
"platforms) trying to access the keychain results in a lot of keychain "
"prompts that require entering your password over and over."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:19
msgid ""
"On macOS this is caused by the development build not having a stable "
"identity. Even if you choose the \"Always Allow\" option, the OS will still "
"prompt you for your password again the next time something changes in the "
"binary."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:23
msgid "This quickly becomes annoying and impedes development speed."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:25
msgid ""
"That is why, by default, when running a development build of Zed an "
"alternative credential provider is used to bypass the system keychain."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:28
msgid ""
"**Note:** This is **only** the case for development builds. For all non-"
"development release channels the system keychain is always used."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:31
msgid ""
"If you need to test something out using the real system keychain in a "
"development build, run Zed with the following environment variable set:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:38
msgid "Performance Measurements"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:40
msgid ""
"Zed includes a frame time measurement system that can be used to profile how "
"long it takes to render each frame. This is particularly useful when "
"comparing rendering performance between different versions or when "
"optimizing frame rendering code."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:42
msgid "Using ZED_MEASUREMENTS"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:44
msgid ""
"To enable performance measurements, set the `ZED_MEASUREMENTS` environment "
"variable:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:47 src/development.md:59
msgid "1"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:50
msgid ""
"When enabled, Zed will print frame rendering timing information to stderr, "
"showing how long each frame takes to render."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:52
msgid "Performance Comparison Workflow"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:54
msgid ""
"Here's a typical workflow for comparing frame rendering performance between "
"different versions:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:56
msgid "**Enable measurements:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:62
msgid "**Test the first version:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:64
msgid "Checkout the commit you want to measure"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:65
msgid ""
"Run Zed in release mode and use it for 5-10 seconds: `cargo run --release &> "
"version-a`"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:67
msgid "**Test the second version:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:69
msgid "Checkout another commit you want to compare"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:70
msgid ""
"Run Zed in release mode and use it for 5-10 seconds: `cargo run --release &> "
"version-b`"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:72
msgid "**Generate comparison:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:78
msgid ""
"The `script/histogram` tool can accept as many measurement files as you like "
"and will generate a histogram visualization comparing the frame rendering "
"performance data between the provided versions."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:80
msgid "Using `util_macros::perf`"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:82
msgid ""
"For benchmarking unit tests, annotate them with the `#[perf]` attribute from "
"the `util_macros` crate. Then run `cargo perf-test -p $CRATE` to benchmark "
"them. See the rustdoc documentation on `crates/util_macros` and `tooling/"
"perf` for in-depth examples and explanations."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:86
msgid "ETW Profiling on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:88
msgid ""
"Zed supports performance profiling with Event Tracing for Windows (ETW) to "
"capture detailed performance data, including CPU, GPU, memory, disk, and "
"file I/O activity. Data is saved to an `.etl` file, which can be opened in "
"standard profiling tools for analysis."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:90
msgid ""
"ETW recordings may contain personally identifiable or security-sensitive "
"information, such as paths to files and registry keys accessed, as well as "
"process names. Please keep this in mind when sharing traces with others."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:92
msgid "Recording a trace"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:94
msgid "Open the command palette and run one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:96
msgid "`zed: record etw trace`: records CPU, GPU, memory, and I/O activity"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:97
msgid ""
"`zed: record etw trace with heap tracing`: includes heap allocation data for "
"the Zed process"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:99
msgid ""
"Zed will prompt you to choose a save location for the `.etl` file, then "
"request administrator permission. Once granted, recording will begin."
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:101
msgid "Saving or canceling"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:103
msgid ""
"While a trace is recording, open the command palette and run one of the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:105
msgid "`zed: save etw trace`: stops recording and saves the trace to disk"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:106
msgid "`zed: cancel etw trace`: stops recording without saving"
msgstr ""

#: src/development.md:108
msgid "Recordings automatically save after 60 seconds if not stopped manually."
msgstr ""

